 "area": "retina",
 "description": "BLOCK 247000 -- BIGVILLE: KNOWN LAWS + IN-GAME ENFORCEMENT. Laws are FIRST-CLASS graph objects the inhabitants HOLD (knows_law), NOT a hardcoded world constraint: a PROHIBITION law (office-holder must not harm a villager from office) John can VIOLATE, and a REMOVAL law (office-holder removable by a council process) that is the LAWFUL instrument of Mary's counter. A violation is WITNESSED (occlusion-gated by the sim's line-of-sight query -- an occluded office scheme is never detected) and decoded against the held law's `prohibits` pattern (bl_detect_violation). Enforcement is an AGENT's decision by rule (bl_enforce/bl_decline): the Constable acts iff its duty outweighs its loyalty to John -- an impartial enforcer censures, a captured one (John's lever) declines, and the violation persists. Mary's reduce_office_hold goal runs THROUGH the removal law: she files a RemovalMotion (bl_mary_files_motion) and the Council removes John only via that motion + impartiality + a believing quorum (bl_council_removes) -- the credibility asymmetry gates the lawful counter. Integer-native (Eq/Gt/And/Not/Plus/IfThenElse/Sum/Exists/NodesOfType/Attr only; no Rust, no new Term). OFF by default: self_load_only, nothing depends_on it, every rule guards on flags (enforce_on/impartial/kind) absent in every existing path. A synthetic MODEL of a law-enforcement mechanism, NOT a claim about real law or politics.",
 "microtheory": {
  "name": "bigville_laws_enforcement",
  "description": "Whether a known law is enforced is set by (a) whether a witnessing enforcer PERCEIVES the violation (occlusion gate), (b) whether the enforcer's own motivation (duty vs loyalty) favours acting, and (c) for removal, whether a lawful motion clears an impartial council at quorum. The law is KNOWN (held by inhabitants); enforcement is an in-game agent decision, never an external referee."
 },
